    "slug": "kahua-card-deck",
    "type": "design",
    "desc": "A physical card deck that reinterprets design thinking through ʻŌiwi epistemology, relationship before solution, kuleana before ideation.",
    "awardNum": "Framework",
    "awardLabel": "ʻŌiwi Design Mindset",
    "category": "Critical Design · Framework",
    "title": "Kahua: An ʻŌiwi Design Thinking Card Deck",
    "subtitle": "A design tool that redesigns how you use design tools.",
    "award": "",
    "liveLink": "/kahua-deck",
    "hasMedia": true,
    "heroImage": "kahua-hero.png",
    "overview": "Kahua means foundation, the ground something is built on. It is a physical card deck derived from the ʻŌiwi Design Mindset, a framework that reinterprets design thinking through Native Hawaiian epistemology. Each card is a prompt that interrupts the default pace of a design process and asks a relational question instead. The deck is a design tool that redesigns how you use design tools.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "title": "The Argument",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "heading": "Design Thinking Has a Worldview",
            "text": "Design thinking, the Stanford d.school model, the IDEO process, the double diamond, is often presented as universal. It is not. Those frameworks embed assumptions about what a problem is, who solves it, what counts as innovation, and how fast thinking should move."
          },
          {
            "heading": "A Different Foundation",
            "text": "Kahua offers a different foundation: relationship before solution, kuleana (responsibility) before ideation, slowing down rather than sprinting, making with rather than making for."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "How It Works",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "heading": "Reframe the Method",
            "text": "Kahua does not argue against design thinking in the abstract, it provides a usable alternative. The deck takes a dominant methodology and rebuilds its foundation on ʻŌiwi values."
          },
          {
            "heading": "Each Card, a Prompt",
            "text": "Every card carries a relational question that slows the process down: relate before you solve, know your kuleana before you ideate. The deck is made to be handled, pulled from, and worked with mid-process."
          },
          {
            "heading": "A Tool for Practitioners",
            "text": "Other designers use the cards not as a passive audience but as active participants, a way into ʻŌiwi epistemology that they bring into their own practice."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Why It Matters",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "heading": "Restoration of Relationship",
            "text": "Kahua is the most directly pedagogical piece in my practice, it teaches. It restores the relationship between design methodology and the cultural knowledge it should be accountable to. Each card is a small act of that restoration: before you solve, relate; before you iterate, slow down; before you make, know your kuleana."
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
